Let me check one detail first — the recipe may still make it *look* interactive.

Good — the recipe carries `cursor: 'pointer'` and `_focusVisible`, which would be stale on an inert element. So option A is four changes, not two.

# Option A, precisely

## 1 · Code — remove the button semantics

```diff
- <chakra.button
-   type="button"
-   aria-label="Change locale"
-   __css={styles.localeButton}
-   // PLACEHOLDER: wire to locale selector once available.
-   onClick={undefined}
- >
+ <chakra.span __css={styles.localeButton}>
    <chakra.span __css={styles.localeText}>United States (USD) / ENG</chakra.span>
    <ZimmIcon type="chevron-right" size="sm" aria-hidden={true} />
- </chakra.button>
+ </chakra.span>
```

## 2 · Recipe — drop the interactive affordances

In `footer.ts`'s `localeButton`: remove `cursor: 'pointer'` and `_focusVisible`. A span can't receive focus, and a pointer cursor on something unclickable is the visual half of the same lie. Everything else (`textStyle`, colour, layout) stays, so **the Figma appearance is unchanged**.

## 3 · Code comment — the why, at the point of use

```tsx
// Country/region selector (REG-020–REG-028) is a separate story: cross-storefront
// switching, region grouping, currency display, search, and the REG-022 basket-loss
// warning. The base LocaleSelector does not apply — it is a within-site *language*
// dropdown gated on supportedLocales.length > 1, and every Zimmermann site has
// exactly one locale, so it would never render.
//
// Non-interactive until that story lands: no button semantics, not focusable, not
// announced as a control.
//
// KNOWN LIMITATION — the label is hardcoded and is only correct on ZimmermannUS.
// It is NOT derivable: CountryConfig has no country display-name field, ZimmermannEU
// serves 4 URL slugs from 1 SFCC site, and per REG-020–028 the displayed country
// reflects the user's *selected* shipping destination — state the unbuilt selector
// owns. Declared in SFCC-55's AC; do not raise as a defect.
```

## 4 · AC line — the actual mitigation

This is the part that does the work, since the code comment isn't visible to QA:

> **Known limitation (SFCC-55):** the footer country/region control renders per Figma but is **non-interactive**, and its label is hardcoded to *"United States (USD) / ENG"* on all storefronts. The country/region selector is **REG-020–REG-028**, a separate story. Label correctness and selector behaviour are **out of scope** and must not be raised as defects against SFCC-55.

---

## What you get

| | Before | After A |
|---|---|---|
| Announced as a control | "Change locale, button" | ✅ nothing |
| Keyboard focusable | yes, Enter does nothing | ✅ no |
| Pointer cursor | yes | ✅ no |
| Focus ring | yes | ✅ no |
| **Figma appearance** | matches | ✅ **unchanged** |
| Label correctness | wrong on 10/11 | ❌ **still wrong on 10/11 — declared** |

Your reasoning holds: A is the only option that keeps the Figma design intact, which matters because design-parity review against Figma is the primary QA surface. B-reduced would have traded 10 content defects for a design-deviation finding on every storefront — swapping one problem for another.

The bet is that **a declared limitation costs less than an undeclared deviation.** That's right, provided the declaration actually lands in the AC — if it stays only in the code comment, you get the 10 defects anyway.

